<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fatherland Front (Austria)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1933">1933</a>–<a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Vaterländische Front</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatherland-front-bulgaria">Fatherland Front</a> was the single ruling party of the authoritarian Federal State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, founded by Chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/engelbert-dollfuss">Engelbert Dollfuss</a> in 1933 after he suspended parliament. Drawing on Catholic corporatism and Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascism">Fascism</a>, it replaced all other parties and opposed both the Social Democrats and the Nazis. It was dissolved after the German annexation of Austria in 1938.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fatherland-front-austria.jpg" alt="Fatherland Front (Austria)" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: W!B · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kruckenkreuz_St%C3%A4ndestaat_%C3%96sterreich.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> three camps shaped the Austrian First Republic: the Social Democrats, the Christian Social Party and the German nationalists. Chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ignaz-seipel">Ignaz Seipel</a>, a proponent of Catholic social teaching, advocated a corporate state above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary system</a>, drawing on the encyclicals Rerum novarum of 1891 and Quadragesimo anno of 1931. On 10 May 1932 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/engelbert-dollfuss">Engelbert Dollfuss</a> became chancellor with a narrow majority, and he used a constitutional crisis in March 1933 to suspend the National Council and rule by decree. On 20 May 1933 he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatherland-front-bulgaria">Fatherland Front</a>, merging his Christian Social Party, the Heimwehr forces and other right-wing groups, to gather all loyal Austrians under one banner.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 30 May 1933 the government banned the paramilitary Republikanischer Schutzbund, and the Communist and Nazi parties were prohibited shortly after. The Schutzbund revolted from 12 February 1934 in the Austrian Civil War against the Heimwehr and the army, and was crushed in a few days; the Social Democratic Party was then dissolved. On 1 May a rump session recast the constitution as authoritarian and corporatist and renamed the country the Federal State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, with the Front as the only legal political organisation. Dollfuss was assassinated in the Nazi July Putsch on 25 July 1934. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurt-schuschnigg">Kurt Schuschnigg</a> became chancellor and took over the Front in 1936.</p><p>On 12 February 1938 Hitler summoned Schuschnigg to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berghof">Berghof</a> and demanded the readmission of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a>. Schuschnigg announced a referendum on independence and agreed to lift the ban on the Social Democrats and their unions in return for support, but he was forced to resign on 11 March 1938. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> was annexed the next day, and the Front was banned two days after that.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Front held a monopoly in politics, with civilian and military divisions. Its symbol was the crutch cross, and its greeting was Österreich! or Front heil!. Membership was obligatory for officials, and by the end of 1937 it had 3 million members among 6.5 million Austrians, but it never became a mass movement. It was backed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>, the bureaucracy, police, military, most of the rural population and a significant part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>'s Jewish community. Historians dispute its character: many call it Austrofascism, while conservative authors stress its defence of independence against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazism</a>. After 1945 former members such as Julius Raab and Leopold Figl founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-peoples-party">Austrian People's Party</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fatherland Front (Austria)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Austrian Civil War of 1934 the government declared martial law, held mass trials, banned the Social Democratic Party and made the <b>Fatherland Front</b> the only legal party.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/conservatism">Conservatism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>The regime ruled through a single party, the <b>Fatherland Front</b>, and corporate bodies in place of a parliament, drawing on the papal encyclical Quadragesimo Anno and on Mussolini's support.</blockquote><a href="/state/federal-state-of-austria">Federal State of Austria</a></li></ul></section>
